# Manners App and Web Platform — Product Summary

The product should be positioned as an interactive manners, etiquette, and cultural-fluency platform for children, teens, adults, parents, teachers, travelers, and professionals. The simplest positioning is “Duolingo for manners and cultural fluency.” Users should be able to learn everyday social skills, dining etiquette, school manners, workplace behavior, digital etiquette, travel etiquette, and country-specific cultural expectations through short interactive lessons rather than long articles or passive videos.

The product should organize cultural content by region for navigation but teach specific behaviors at the country and situation level. Broad labels such as “Asian manners” or “Middle Eastern manners” are too general to teach accurately. Every cultural lesson should identify the country, situation, expected behavior, context, formality level, strength of the norm, exceptions, safest action when unsure, cultural reasoning, applicable age groups, sources, reviewer, and last review date. This prevents stereotypes and allows the content to be updated as customs change.

The first major curriculum should be a Global Manners Core covering polite basics; greetings and introductions; conversation; listening; interruptions; respect; kindness; gratitude; personal boundaries; apologies; home and family behavior; hosting; being a guest; school etiquette; friendship; table manners; restaurant etiquette; public spaces; transportation; phone behavior; texting; group chats; social media; gaming; video calls; workplace etiquette; professional communication; travel; cultural customs; religious and sacred spaces; weddings; birthdays; funerals; hygiene; accessibility etiquette; conflict resolution; sportsmanship; neighbor etiquette; community behavior; and environmental manners. Every category should contain free lessons so users can learn basic manners without paying.

Lessons should adapt by age instead of simply changing the artwork. Ages 5–7 should focus on simple concrete actions with voice narration and very little reading. Ages 8–12 should add social reasoning, empathy, friendships, school situations, and consequences. Teens should include social media, group chats, dating boundaries, gossip, parties, teachers, first jobs, conflict, and reputation. Adults should focus more heavily on professional communication, restaurants, travel, international etiquette, dating, events, hosting, business behavior, and workplace situations.

The first international launch should not attempt to cover the entire world. Start with a Global Core and approximately eight country packs such as the United States, United Kingdom, Mexico, Brazil, Japan, South Korea, India, and United Arab Emirates. Later expand into countries such as Canada, France, Germany, Spain, Italy, China, Singapore, Thailand, Saudi Arabia, Australia, New Zealand, Nigeria, and South Africa. Regional pages should help users browse North America, Latin America and the Caribbean, Europe, the Middle East and North Africa, Sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia, East Asia, Southeast Asia, and Oceania, while the actual instruction remains country-specific.

Every lesson should follow a short interactive learning loop: watch a situation, choose what to do, see the consequence, learn the reason, retry the scenario, and receive a real-world challenge. A user might watch someone entering a Japanese home, choose whether to keep their shoes on, remove them, or ask the host, then immediately see the reaction and receive a short explanation. The lesson should explain not only what is commonly expected but why, when exceptions occur, and what to do when unsure. The app should teach judgment rather than memorizing rigid rules.

Gamification should reward learning and real-life behavior rather than meaningless tapping. Features can include a Manners Passport with country stamps, daily scenarios, mastery levels, kindness streaks, Culture Explorer achievements, review sessions for missed scenarios, family challenges, and real-world missions. Progress levels could move from Beginner to Comfortable, Confident, and Ambassador. Real-world missions might ask users to thank someone, introduce themselves properly, practice listening, put phones away during dinner, or use a learned cultural behavior.

Animation should be central to the experience, but AI video should not power every interaction. Seedance 2.5 or similar video-generation tools can create short cinematic scenes from consistent illustrations. A workflow could begin with a carefully designed static image showing characters, environment, expressions, and props. That image can then be sent into a video-generation model with instructions to create a subtle 5–10 second animation while maintaining character consistency, camera position, art style, and scene layout. The app should overlay questions and interface elements separately so the same animation can work across languages.

The main interactive animation system should preferably use Rive. Characters and environments can be designed with reusable animation states such as idle, talking, listening, walking, greeting, smiling, apologizing, giving, receiving, eating, pointing, confused, embarrassed, angry, thanking, and asking permission. The application can trigger these animations instantly depending on the user's choice. This is more efficient and interactive than generating a separate video for every possible response.

Lottie should be used for small interface animations such as stars, badges, checkmarks, transitions, progress rewards, and confetti. PixiJS can support advanced custom 2D interactions. Phaser can be used when a lesson becomes a true educational mini-game. GSAP can power sophisticated web animation, and Spline can be explored for limited interactive 3D experiences. Raw HTML Canvas should only be used when these tools cannot provide the required interaction.

The animation production system should use reusable character poses, environments, and props rather than creating every lesson from scratch. Character animation libraries should include common social expressions and actions. Reusable environments should include homes, classrooms, restaurants, streets, trains, airports, offices, birthday parties, hotels, stores, friends' homes, and religious spaces. Props should include phones, plates, utensils, chopsticks, gifts, backpacks, shoes, doors, chairs, and business cards. The goal is to create a digital puppet-theater system where content designers can assemble scenes quickly.

An internal lesson-authoring platform should eventually become a major part of the product. A creator should be able to choose the country, category, age range, environment, characters, prompt, answers, correct response, explanation, animation state, sources, cultural reviewer, and review date, then publish the lesson without requiring a software update. Cultural content should function as structured data so adding a new country or updating etiquette does not require rebuilding the app.

A practical technical stack would be React Native with Expo for the mobile applications, Next.js for the web product and public SEO pages, TypeScript for shared application logic, Rive for interactive animation, a Postgres-based backend such as Supabase, object storage and CDN delivery for media, a headless content-management system for lessons, RevenueCat or equivalent subscription infrastructure for cross-platform entitlements, app-store billing on mobile, and normal web payments for browser customers. AI video tools should be treated primarily as offline content-production tools rather than runtime dependencies.

The public website should also function as a search engine acquisition channel. Pages should exist for subjects such as Japan manners, Japanese dining etiquette, Japanese train etiquette, restaurant manners, kids' manners, workplace etiquette, and similar high-intent searches. Each page should provide useful free information and lead directly into an interactive lesson. Educational content should therefore serve both the learning product and customer acquisition.

The business model should use freemium rather than advertising. Advertising is a poor fit for a product involving children, privacy, education, and parent trust. The free version should be genuinely useful instead of being a demo. It should include approximately 50–75 Global Core lessons, at least one lesson from every manners category, country overview pages, basic lessons from every supported region, a daily scenario, and basic progress tracking. Basic manners education should remain free permanently.

The main paid subscription can be called Manners+ or similar. Initial pricing could be tested around $6.99 per month or approximately $49.99–$59.99 per year. The subscription should unlock the full lesson library, every country, advanced scenarios, personalized review, offline lessons, travel mode, professional etiquette, advanced cultural modules, certificates, and unlimited practice. Pricing should be tested and adjusted rather than treated as permanent.

A Family plan could cost approximately $79.99 per year and support several profiles. Parents could assign lessons, see general learning progress, and issue real-world challenges without turning the product into invasive surveillance. Family learning should emphasize shared practice such as dinner manners, introductions, gratitude, hosting, conflict resolution, and phone behavior.

The company should also sell one-time Travel Packs for adults who do not want a subscription. A traveler going to Japan, for example, could purchase a Japan Etiquette Pack for approximately $9.99 containing greetings, trains, restaurants, shoes, onsen behavior, shrines and temples, tipping, photography, hotels and ryokan, dining, common mistakes, basic business etiquette, polite phrases, and emergency situations. Country travel packs have strong search intent and allow the company to monetize users who only need temporary cultural guidance.

Schools should become a separate major business line. A classroom product could provide curriculum by grade level, teacher assignment tools, progress reporting, digital citizenship lessons, respect and boundaries, conversation skills, dining etiquette, cultural awareness, conflict resolution, and social-emotional situations. Pricing could eventually be offered per classroom, teacher, student, school, or district. The same platform could later be licensed to hotels, airlines, international schools, universities, study-abroad programs, corporate relocation companies, hospitality organizations, and multinational employers.

The long-term revenue model should therefore be free education at the base, consumer subscriptions for depth, family subscriptions, one-time travel packs, school licensing, and business or institutional licensing. A hard paywall may produce higher short-term conversion, but maintaining useful free access creates a stronger mission, broader audience, better organic acquisition, and greater trust. Paid products should monetize depth, personalization, advanced applications, institutional use, and convenience rather than locking away basic social skills.

A realistic MVP should contain approximately 60 Global Core lessons and eight country packs with roughly 10 lessons each, for about 140 interactive lessons. It should include around 12 reusable environments, approximately eight reusable characters, 20–30 common character animations, one reusable Rive interaction system, a quiz and scenario engine, spaced review, the Manners Passport, one subscription tier, and one test Travel Pack. This is enough content to validate demand without creating hundreds of lessons before product-market fit is known.

Every cultural lesson should clearly answer three questions: what is commonly expected, why it is expected, and what the user should do when unsure. The product should never present cultural etiquette as universal or absolute when it is not. This system makes the app more accurate, respectful, practical, and globally scalable.

The final positioning should focus on confidence rather than “proper behavior.” For children, the promise is, “I know what to do.” For parents, it is, “I am teaching my child useful life skills.” For travelers, it is, “I can respect local customs and avoid unnecessary mistakes.” For adults and professionals, it is, “I can handle unfamiliar social situations confidently.” The strongest product is therefore not simply a manners app. It is a global social-skills, etiquette, and cultural-confidence learning platform.
